<div id="shopify-section-sections--19674847871158__header" class="vy-vy-vy-shopify-section vy-vy-vy-shopify-section-group-header-group vy-vy-vy-section-header"> <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="vy-vy-vy-hidden"><use href="#vsym-20" xlink:href="#vsym-20"></use></svg> </div> <main id="MainContent" class="vy-vy-vy-content-for-layout vy-vy-vy-focus-none" role="main" tabindex="-1"> <div id="replo-fullpage-element" data-runtime-version="daefba70-f888-11f0-bde4-23589a709ef8" style="display: block !important" data-alchemy-element-root="65aa4fd9-9605-4f13-ad87-8e02eadd644d"> <div data-alchemy-element-mount-node="true"><div class="vy-vy-vy-alchemy__element vy-vy-vy-alchemy-reset vy-vy-vy-overflow-clip"><div data-rid="8cf2f58b-a2e3-46f7-b728-73a457a025f4" class="vy-vy-vy-r-a9c4zn"><div data-rid="e4549197-1b9c-4e1c-912d-c642aa52ca07" class="vy-vy-vy-r-z0jkw8">
<div data-rid="87b03e09-c60f-47d2-bff5-5b7f9d9b0935" class="vy-vy-vy-r-kbnevj">
<div data-rid="4d739a2d-1b2a-4992-b74b-67f333fbae8f" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1p6kmfd">
<!-- breadcrumb removed -->
<div data-rid="b363fe84-8f40-46c4-b3db-af0371cf2201" class="vy-vy-vy-r-ijaxxl">
<div data-rid="19b5ea51-d876-4c97-8bac-11997080d67b" class="vy-vy-vy-r-73kd2d vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h1>At 68, I'd almost given up finding a gift my grandson would actually use. Everyone told me to just buy him another screen. Six weeks after I handed him one hardcover book, he still hasn't put it down.</h1></span></div>
<div data-rid="5f91f721-34ae-4c63-984e-b662f2139423" class="vy-vy-vy-r-t9cuvq vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>For months I searched for a present that wouldn't be forgotten by New Year. <mark data-color="#e9f5fcFF" style="background-color: #e9f5fcFF; color: inherit">Then a friend said over coffee: give him this one book and just watch what happens.</mark> Today it lives on his nightstand, and he reads it every single evening.</p></span></div>
</div>
</div>
<div data-rid="3428a382-d4a2-46d4-aeb1-5e9f10595452" class="vy-vy-vy-r-6ar4dq"><picture data-rid="07254baf-13c1-4f73-b9d1-da0c977cdd7a" class="vy-vy-vy-r-7nqvm4"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-3.jpg" media="(max-width: 640px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-3.jpg" media="(min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 1024px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-3.jpg" media="(min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 2400px)"><img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-3.jpg" class="vy-vy-vy-r-wgidcy" loading="eager"></picture></div>
<div data-rid="1d5b2839-8135-41b1-9169-5384832138a3" class="vy-vy-vy-r-scmfee"><div data-rid="3fe0a971-08e0-432b-8569-74f0ac0e2100" class="vy-vy-vy-r-12357h vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p><strong>I'm 68, and I can tell you the exact evening I stopped guessing. My grandson opened yet another gadget at his birthday, smiled for about a minute, and had wandered off to something else before the cake was even cut.</strong></p>
<p><br><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">It hadn't always felt this hard. But somewhere along the way every present I picked turned into another toy that lit up for a week and then sat in a bin. Noah is eight, sharp as anything and full of questions, and I kept buying him things that asked nothing of him at all.</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">I finally asked his mother what he actually needed, half certain she'd say a new game console. She went quiet, then said the thing I didn't want to hear: he's on a screen most of the day, and nothing we've tried gets him to sit with a book.</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)"><strong>So that's exactly what I did for a while. I kept buying the easy gifts. And every time Noah's eyes drifted back to a tablet, part of me ached, because I remembered a boy who used to ask me why the sky was blue and actually wait for the answer.</strong></span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">Things had gotten quieter with him. Shorter visits, more headphones, fewer of those endless 'but why, Grandma?' questions I used to pretend to be tired of. I told myself this was simply what kids are like now. Deep down I would have given anything to get his curiosity back.</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">Then I met Diane for coffee. We've been friends for years. She's a grandmother too, practical and funny, the sort who researches everything before she buys it. She took one look at me across the table and asked what was wrong.</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">"Why do you look like you've lost something?" she asked, stirring her coffee and waiting me out.</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">I told her what Noah's mother had said, and my voice wobbled more than I expected.</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">"He's on a screen all day and nobody can pull him off it. I miss the little boy who used to ask me a hundred questions. I don't know what you're supposed to do with 'that's just kids now.'"</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">She set her cup down and nodded, like she'd heard the very same worry from a dozen grandparents before me. Then she said the thing that stuck:</span><br><br></p>
<p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0)">"I was exactly where you are last year with my granddaughter. There's one book I gave her, the Kid's Encyclopedia '10,000 Whys.' Give it to Noah and watch him for a couple of weeks. After that you can tell me screens were the only thing that held him."</span></p></span></div></div>
<div data-rid="f9ed0282-e79d-4b58-869c-4c65411c8295" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1o66a95">
<div data-rid="6514d256-32c8-41c8-a2d4-2498a6c46944" class="vy-vy-vy-r-48xodv"><div data-rid="3b7f3f34-290f-4e0c-bb83-b1c4c14ab7f3" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1assrdo vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h2>The coffee where Diane told me about the book</h2></span></div></div>
<div data-rid="102db264-f288-4e13-9fb7-a1979fea3774" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1utup6c"><picture data-rid="db2bbd37-1ae9-4deb-b493-8379e1525ccb" class="vy-vy-vy-r-vmdqyv"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-4.jpg" media="(max-width: 640px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-4.jpg" media="(min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 1024px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-4.jpg" media="(min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 2400px)"><img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-4.jpg" alt="Two grandmothers talking over coffee about the Kid's Encyclopedia "10,000 Whys."" class="vy-vy-vy-r-9vp9sy" loading="eager"></picture></div>
<div data-rid="416b4835-b77b-4e31-be11-5b8d5eb6e2fb" class="vy-vy-vy-r-103iwmh"><div data-rid="992cbe25-88fb-49a4-b711-6f3fff67feac" class="vy-vy-vy-r-5ybhrv vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Diane pulled out her phone right there at the table and showed me the page. One thick hardcover encyclopedia written for kids, packed with the real questions children actually ask. No app, no screen, nothing to charge. He just opens it and reads.<br><br></p>
<p>Then she read me what it covers. Space and the universe, dinosaurs, the human body, oceans, weather, animals, history, science, all of it in one book, written simply and illustrated on every page. I went quiet for a moment.<br><br></p>
<p>"This is the one thing that works with my granddaughter," she said. "The '10,000 Whys.' It answers the questions kids really wonder about, in language they get, so they keep turning pages instead of asking for the tablet. Built for exactly the age Noah is. <strong>No screen, no batteries, and it's his to keep forever.</strong>"<br><br></p>
<p>I looked at her. "Diane, it's a book. He has a whole shelf of books he's never opened."<br><br></p>
<p>She just smiled and kept scrolling. "Not like this one. Give it to him and pay attention to the first week. <strong>You won't need me to talk you into buying a second copy for the other grandkids.</strong>"<br><br></p>
<p>I was skeptical. I'd already handed him plenty of 'educational' toys that thrilled him for a day and changed nothing, and I'd long stopped expecting much from a shiny product page.<br><br></p>
<p>But Diane had already sent me the link, so I ordered it that same evening.</p></span></div></div>
</div>
<div data-rid="48c0f854-e5f2-4779-bb4b-55cf3d91f90e" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1r3euik">
<div data-rid="a5848c73-1ab7-4c3e-a2e1-9afff9e67c49" class="vy-vy-vy-r-12rzfy5"><div data-rid="0dca9c46-58f1-4559-983d-03765a3874ca" class="vy-vy-vy-r-l3hav7 vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h2>The first evenings, when he actually chose the book</h2></span></div></div>
<div data-rid="9d62fcf2-265d-4b5c-82c6-d2388dfccd38" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1ph6ghk"><picture data-rid="348c8797-f352-4b23-ab18-9232ba342d87" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1lkr9e1"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-5.jpg" media="(max-width: 640px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-5.jpg" media="(min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 1024px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-5.jpg" media="(min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 2400px)"><img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-5.jpg" class="vy-vy-vy-r-8o2viz" loading="eager"></picture></div>
<div data-rid="3f0384b0-ede1-4738-8f01-7b979c5cd77d" class="vy-vy-vy-r-aeqb59"><div data-rid="50bd4e06-3f8c-4da1-9719-0f816836291f" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1t1t72j vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>The package came a few days later. A solid, satisfying hardcover, heavier than I expected, with a cover so colorful Noah grabbed it out of the box before I could even wrap it.<br><br></p>
<p>Inside were the things children genuinely wonder about, laid out page by page with big illustrations. Nothing dense, nothing dumbed down. Just answers to the questions he was already asking.<br><br></p>
<p>I braced for the usual story anyway: a polite thank you, one flip through, then back to the screen by bedtime. Diane had said most grandparents are surprised by how fast that first evening goes.<br><br></p>
<p>She was right. He opened to a page about black holes, then one about why the ocean is salty, and forgot I was in the room. When his mother called him for dinner he asked for five more minutes.<br><br></p>
<p>The next evening, the same. Homework done, then straight to the book on the floor with his knees up.<br><br></p>
<p>By the third or fourth evening I caught him reading it after lights-out with a flashlight, the way I used to hide under the covers with a torch.<br><br></p>
<p><strong>No app to download. No battery to charge. Just one book that quietly became the part of his day he looked forward to most.</strong><br><br></p>
<p>I texted Diane: "Did I seriously just catch him reading past his bedtime, on purpose?"<br><br></p>
<p>She texted back: "That's the whole point. <strong>It feels like fun to him, not homework.</strong> That's why kids actually stick with it."<br><br></p>
<p>That night I sat on the edge of his bed for a minute after he'd finally fallen asleep, book still open on the covers.<br><br></p>
<p>I felt... hopeful. Not because of one gift. Because the boy who used to ask me everything was clearly still in there.<br><br></p>
<p>The ease was <strong>real.</strong><br><br></p>
<p>He told his mother three facts about the moon at breakfast, the way he used to chatter before the screens took over. And the quiet worry I'd been carrying for two years <strong>suddenly felt lighter.</strong><br><br></p>
<p>I felt like a grandmother who'd finally found the right gift.<br><br></p>
<p><strong>And that was DAY THREE.</strong></p></span></div></div>
</div>
<div data-rid="0f79d102-7685-427a-97f7-384a156284fb" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1i5xl6o">
<div data-rid="5df61287-3519-4c5d-ad58-2c1e2a620960" class="vy-vy-vy-r-w8aq40"><div data-rid="aaf0257d-e3ab-403e-8873-4a95c1b96c14" class="vy-vy-vy-r-aifh4o vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h2>Why it worked when the toys and apps didn't</h2></span></div></div>
<div data-rid="29bbe301-0046-4fcd-a683-4f1d88ff5a93" class="vy-vy-vy-r-g2lqx3"><picture data-rid="55f62894-04e3-4e79-8438-b1c51a597af6" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1pahr4a"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-6.jpg" media="(max-width: 640px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-6.jpg" media="(min-width: 641px) and (max-width: 1024px)"><source srcset="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-6.jpg" media="(min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 2400px)"><img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-339-prime-naturals-shopify/images/img-6.jpg" alt="A curious child smiling, confident and full of questions after weeks with the "10,000 Whys" book." class="vy-vy-vy-r-1w41bw4" loading="eager"></picture></div>
<div data-rid="a0de0f9b-1c35-410e-92c7-6f088c2f7ea9" class="vy-vy-vy-r-13zo5va">
<div data-rid="8973131b-19ac-49fa-b7c1-f548594cda1b" class="vy-vy-vy-r-x20i5p vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>After a couple of weeks I wanted to understand why one book was doing what a shelf of gadgets never had. I called Diane and made her explain the whole thing.<br><br></p>
<p>What she told me honestly made me a little cross, mostly about every overpriced 'educational' toy I'd bought that never had a chance of keeping him.</p></span></div>
<div data-rid="8dc0fcbd-8ca6-4b7d-9931-11042c6df7b9" class="vy-vy-vy-r-13v77ik">
<div data-rid="e951b7e3-6f6b-40fc-a0ae-6861e4f80c0d" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1kr1ha8 vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h3>Why the usual gifts fall flat</h3></span></div>
<div data-rid="04f020c6-c4be-47db-b602-d5b222bf892d" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1az2u1r vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p><strong>Most of the time the advice is buy him whatever's popular, or download another 'learning' app. It looks fine on the shelf, but it does nothing for a curious mind tonight, and a flashy app only holds his attention for a few minutes before he taps away to a game.</strong><br><br></p>
<p>The other route is the enrichment treadmill. Tutoring and after-school programs add up fast, and they mean schedules, drop-offs, and turning something a child does for fun into one more thing they have to attend.<br><br></p>
<p>So you feel stuck between handing him another screen and signing him up for programs that cost far more than a book and drain the joy out of learning. In between, there was nothing that quietly fed his curiosity while he simply enjoyed it.</p></span></div>
</div>
<div data-rid="68277b63-d7c1-49a1-903a-170eda2c6f6a" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1fpj5st">
<div data-rid="2e9108a1-7ddf-4113-8e66-17acb56c4233" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1gqkxaz vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h3>Why one good book works differently</h3></span></div>
<div data-rid="048301db-ead5-49be-b9f4-c1c3e847fece" class="vy-vy-vy-r-1mtlaty vy-vy-vy-alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Diane explained it to me like this:<br><br></p>
